{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790966804,"nonce":"g38KKMmsnPUgDKG5G0oML/d4ozs=","title":"Text soft on standard-DPI (1x) displays, crisp on retina (2x): glyph quad origins were","message":"Text (and Linear-sampled SVG/images) looked soft on a standard-DPI / non-retina monitor (`scale_factor ~1.0`) but crisp on retina (`~2.0`).\n\n**Root cause.** The glyph quad is 1:1 with the atlas bitmap (`quad w/h == atlas region w/h`) and per-glyph advances/bearings are integer-rounded at rasterization, so the ONLY fractional part of a glyph's device-pixel position is the fractional part of the text ORIGIN — which is routinely fractional (fractional layout x/y from gaps/padding/1px borders/flex centering, plus the structurally-fractional `(line_height - cap_height)/2` vertical centering). The glyph atlas sampler is `FilterMode::Linear` (blinc_gpu text.rs) and the SDF text shader assumes fragments hit texel centers (integer+0.5) — only true if the quad origin is integer-aligned. Unsnapped, Linear smears each glyph across ~1 device px. At 2x that error halves visually and usually lands back on integers (looks sharp); at 1x every glyph samples between texels.\n\n**Fix.** Round the glyph origin to the physical pixel grid at the non-transformed glyph-emit paths in context.rs (`for glyph in \u0026mut glyphs { glyph.bounds[0]=..round(); glyph.bounds[1]=..round(); }` before `all_glyphs.extend` AND before `glyphs_by_layer...extend` — there are TWO parallel emit paths, the DrawContext-dispatch `all_glyphs` and the compositor-cache `glyphs_by_layer`). Because quad w/h and advances are already integers, snapping the origin makes every quad span an exact integer pixel range with no cumulative drift. Do NOT snap the `css_affine` / transformed branches — animated/rotated/scaled text needs smooth sub-pixel motion; its bounds are recomputed by the affine anyway. Commit `287e53df`. Secondary: SVG/image dest rects use the same Linear sampler and soften the same way at 1x — snapping their dest rects would sharpen them too (not done; text was the reported symptom, though the user observed SVGs improved as well).","files":null}]}